Dr Darth schrieb:

[Just up front: i have my reservations - the wizardly vs. priestly
spell "realms" look too much like introducing an AD&Dism for me to
readily agree]

> I read quite a few articles in this group about class differentiation,
> or lack of it, in Nethack.  One solution that occurred to me is to
> have two parallel systems of magic. 

Currently, magic-use already is one of the more significant
differentiations between classes, moderated by general ability (a.k.a.
fail rates), starting attributes, spellcasting skills, equipment
restrictions and power reserves. 

> For shorthand, let's call them
> WizardLike (WL) and PriestLike (PL), and I'll explain my idea:

On a historical basis, one could argue that priests and sorcerers were
not really different in their alleged relationship with the
supernatural, but rather in their social role. But as much as
NHINADND, NHINRL, either.

> Gaining new spells under the WL system would involve reading books.
> Wizards are studious people who like nothing better that burying
> themselves in large, dusty, obscure volumes for hours on end.

Well, priests often get a lot of their sacred knowledge from
scripture, too, i should think.

> The PL system would involve being granted the ability to perform a
> particular spell by your god.  This would occur on level-up/from
> sacrificing/from praying (delete or alter as appropriate).

Hmmm. Would this be random or pre-determined? I think the "spell
acquirement" of your priestly magic will be difficult to balance -
currently, you get a somewhat random number of spellbooks by simply
clearing out the dungeon, which _usually_ provides enough spells to
keep a wizard going (and new books can be written on blanked ones). In
addition, wishes and prayers can provide even more.
If "priestly" spells could only be had from levels and specific
god-related activities, it could end up granting too few "reliable"
spells, _and_ it would tend to encourage sacrifice-fests.

> Once the
> ability to perform the spell is granted, it is never forgotten (the
> gift of a divine being is by definition perfect - it would be
> sacrilege to suggest otherwise).

But what happens if the beneficiary of the gift falls from grace or
even converts? I would think that in the first case (low alignment,
angry god), the ability would need to be suspended until atonement is
made. In the case of conversion, all spells granted by the old god
would necessarily be lost. 

> So, there we go.  It's a little radical, it needs refinement and
> further thought, but it's a start.  I'll be fascinated to read your
> suggestions and reactions.

I'm not sure that anyone's going to make a patch as it stands, but i
think the idea in general has potential.

